"A data vault diagram separates business identities, their relationships, and descriptive history into hubs, links, and satellites.\n\nUse when: Integrate changing source systems with historical traceability. Explain raw-vault structure and source provenance.\n\nAvoid when: Readers need a simple reporting star or operational transaction schema.\n\nVisual grammar: Distinct shapes or labeled headers identify hubs, links, and satellites. Hubs show business keys; links connect hub identities. Satellites attach descriptive history and source metadata to hubs or links.\n\nBuild: Identify stable business keys and their source qualifications. Create links for the relationships being recorded. Group descriptive history into satellites with coherent loading rules. Place hubs and links on the main spine and satellites beside their owner with padded spacing.\n\nCheck: A satellite has a clear parent and history grain. Load metadata and source identity remain traceable. Hash keys, when used, are distinguished from business keys. Satellite connectors avoid hub key text and link labels.\n\nPitfalls: Using hubs as fact tables and satellites as ordinary dimensions. Claiming a complete methodology from a topology alone.\n\nExample: Customer and Order hubs connect through a CustomerOrder link; separate source satellites preserve descriptive changes.\n\nAlternatives: Dimensional schema diagram, Data lineage diagram."
